WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.541
A program lives

00:00:01.352 --> 00:00:02.472
in people's heads

00:00:03.006 --> 00:00:04.539
In nineteen eighty-five

00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:05.930
Peter Naur argued

00:00:05.742 --> 00:00:06.732
that a program

00:00:06.579 --> 00:00:07.428
is mostly a

00:00:07.239 --> 00:00:08.289
theory held by

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:08.831
the people who

00:00:08.643 --> 00:00:09.279
build it

00:00:09.504 --> 00:00:10.388
not the code

00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:11.213
or the docs

00:00:11.734 --> 00:00:13.113
A program dies

00:00:12.937 --> 00:00:13.691
he wrote

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:14.611
when the team

00:00:14.423 --> 00:00:15.495
holding its theory

00:00:15.307 --> 00:00:16.120
dissolves

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:17.049
It may keep

00:00:16.861 --> 00:00:18.228
running but nobody

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:18.830
can change it

00:00:18.642 --> 00:00:19.408
safely

00:00:19.934 --> 00:00:21.183
Coding agents fit

00:00:20.995 --> 00:00:21.679
the pattern

00:00:21.868 --> 00:00:22.634
they can read

00:00:22.505 --> 00:00:23.448
every file

00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:24.698
yet the understanding

00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:25.617
they build lasts

00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:26.596
only as long

00:00:26.408 --> 00:00:27.351
as the session

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:28.884
Full story on

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:29.591
the dashboard
